From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 12:49:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rebuild My team tree as a role-tier grid instead of
 depth-nested list
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous tree indented strictly by reports_to depth, so a Sales
Associate who reports straight to a Senior Sales Executive rendered at
the same visual level as their direct manager, and a same-role peer one
hop further down (e.g. via a Senior Sales Associate) rendered one row
lower — depth and seniority don't always match.

Replace it with a grid layout: row = job-role tier (every Senior Sales
Associate lands in the same row regardless of who they report to),
column = a standard tidy-tree position derived from the actual
reports_to edges (leaves get sequential slots, a parent centers over its
own children). Because columns are assigned uniquely in one pass, a
connector line dropped straight down a node's own column never crosses
an unrelated card even when it has to pass through a row where that
node's real manager isn't — so someone whose manager is two tiers up
just gets a line that skips the row in between, landing them next to
their actual peers instead of alone under their manager.

Confirmed against the reference case (Kishan / Natthi / Pradeep /
Harish / Deepak) with a standalone script mirroring computeLayout()
exactly — output matches the approved mockup: Deepak grid-right of
Harish, skip-level dashed line straight to Kishan.

